{"reference":"Esther 5","book":"Esther","chapter":5,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Esther’s Approach","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Esther’s appearance before the king follows Mordecai’s warning that silence would not secure her safety. She had called the Jews of Susa to fast for three days and fasted with her young women before taking the risk of approaching the king uninvited. That background gives added weight to the king’s favorable response and to Esther’s careful, delayed presentation of her request.","verse_refs":["Esther 5:1-8"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_esther_005_12664f219f9230ea712a54f2"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading Esther as a Whole","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Readers differ over how to classify Esther as a whole. The story combines informed Persian coloring with stylization, satire, repeated improbabilities, exact symmetry, and dramatic reversals; labels such as history, historical novella, comedy, court tale, and festival etiology highlight different features rather than settling every historical question. Readers also encounter materially different forms of Esther: Greek versions expand and interpret the narrative through dreams, prayers, divine naming, and fuller documents.","verse_refs":["Esther 10:1-3","Esther 1:1-22","Esther 2:1-23","Esther 3:1-15","Esther 4:1-17","Esther 6:1-14","Esther 7:1-10","Esther 8:1-17","Esther 9:1-32"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_esther_005_c2117ab014db89a1853a224e"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}